Kartuli Vaporstep

Ingredients: GEORGIAN POLYPHONIC VOCALS + DUB TECHNO + UK GARAGE + BASS CLARINET + PREPARED PIANO + 2020s ERA

Creative Catalyst: The genre began when mountain choir recordings were discovered on damaged cassettes inside an abandoned hydroelectric control room, where the tape wow happened to lock rhythmically with the building's relay clicks and turbine hum.

Kartuli Vaporstep is a low-lit hybrid of Georgian polyphonic singing, dub-techno spatial design, and clipped UK-garage rhythm. Its defining tension comes from voices that feel ancient and physically communal set against drums that feel microscopic, syncopated, and machine-cut. The choir is not used as cinematic ornament: stacked open fifths, seconds, drones, and rough parallel motion determine the harmony. Bass clarinet carries the low melodic voice, often shadowing the bassline before peeling away into woody multiphonics. Prepared piano adds dry, unstable attacks that bridge human breath and electronic percussion.

Genre DNA
* Tempo & Pulse: 128–134 BPM, usually 4/4, with a two-step garage skeleton: displaced kick, clipped snare on backbeat anchors, swung sixteenth hats, and periodic 5-beat vocal phrases spilling across barlines.
* Harmonic Palette: Open fifth drones, minor seconds, suspended fourths, flattened sixth motion, and slowly shifting modal centers. Triadic closure is deliberately delayed; the choir often holds unresolved interval stacks while dub chords imply the tonal destination.
* Production Signatures: 1. Stone-Chamber Send — choir stems feed very long dark reverbs while consonants stay mostly dry, creating the impression of voices inside a massive masonry chamber. 2. Relay-Swing — relay-click field recordings are quantized only partially, then layered under garage hats to introduce human-machine timing friction. 3. Clarinet Pressure Bloom — bass clarinet multiphonics trigger sidechain expansion on dub chords so harmonic space appears to widen around breath. 4. Prepared-Key Ghosting — muted and screwed piano attacks are reversed into snare tails and microfills.
* Dynamics: Verses are sparse and close-miked, often bass clarinet, sub, prepared piano, and one vocal drone. Choruses become wider rather than louder, opening into three-part male/female choral stacks, deep dub echoes, and stereo chord clouds. Breakdowns strip to naked voices and mechanical room tone before the rhythm re-enters half a beat late.

Scene & Origin Lore
The style formed around a decommissioned hydroelectric station in the Caucasus that had been converted into an informal rehearsal and archive space. Engineers clearing the control room found several unlabeled cassettes containing village singers recorded decades earlier. The tapes were warped by heat and humidity, producing slow pitch drift. A producer looped one damaged phrase against the surviving electromechanical relay bank; the relay chatter accidentally created a swung pulse.

Local singers began visiting the station to identify the melodies. Instead of restoring the recordings to clean archival form, they performed alongside them, treating tape damage as another voice. London-based electronic musicians passing through the project later introduced two-step drums and dub mixing techniques. The resulting sessions became known as "pressure vigils": performances held after dark with most stage lighting switched off, leaving only control-panel lamps and the red glow of tape-machine meters.

